A||||27||Seanh||Beretta Brake Upgrade ?'s||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||10-27-2000||04:33 PM||||I was going to use the Chris West kit, like $1200 for the kit. I saw a site the other day telling the process for doing the beretta brake upgrade. I think to save a little money I might just do the chris west on the rear, and beretta's on the front. Sound good? Is there any bad things to the beretta's other then the all the work to doing them and the wheel setting out 9mm more then stock? Is that 9mm a problem? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||10-27-2000||05:43 PM||||Why in the world would you want to put West Coast Fiero brakes on the rear. The Grand Am/Beretta brake swap is nearly a bolt on  for the rear and vastly cheaper than what Chris West's brakes cost. Hope he's gotten faster at shipping things out than he used to be. Once the front hubs are done, the front is not hard either.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||10-27-2000||06:12 PM||||It was my understanding that the Grand Am/Beretta's would not allow for the e-brake. I found I can put an in-line booster to lock the rear brakes, but it is only a parking brake, not an e-brake. I wasnt really looking forward to doing that, this is why I want Chris West brakes on the rear. Do you know of a way to use the factory e-brake system with the Beretta brakes?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||10-27-2000||06:26 PM||||My brother has the Beretta brakes on his fiero and I have to tell you they are awesome. After installing those brakes you won't have to do the rears. He got them from the fiero store as a kit.||||reg||1
Z||000004||robertjay||10-27-2000||07:49 PM||||There is a gentleman by the name of Walt Zettner that did a ton of research and found rear brake calipers that still use the E-Brake cable.  His instructions call for 11.25" vented rotors too.  If you want, I don't exactly know where his web page is, but I saved it to my harddrive, e-mail me and I will send it to you.  It's real informative, and fairly inexpensive for what you get in return.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||10-27-2000||07:58 PM||||You want or need the e-brake, of course, I forget some people actually use them or live in areas where "big brother" forces you to have your vehicle inspected.<P>Another option is to just change the front (which does the greatest share of braking anyway) and install an adjustable proportioning valve to fine tune your brake system.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||10-27-2000||08:51 PM||||I was also thinking about doing that. I would probally replace the rear rotors with cross drilled ones, and get some good brake pads. I was wondering, with the beretta brakes on the front, would the stock MC work okay? I was reading that I probally should upgrade it to a MC from an S-10. But, I am wondering, should I upgrade if I stay with the rear stock brakes? <P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
