Z||000009||bHooper||10-27-2000||09:23 PM||||I haven't had time to look at the rear fiero calipers, much less a grand am calliper, but does anyone think it would be possible to modify it in some way to add parking breaks?<P>Seanh... please explain "in-line booster", and how this affects the rear breaks.  Thanks!<P>I have thought of useing either fierostores GA brake kit, or spening the money on the RCC kit.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||10-27-2000||10:58 PM||||I bet Sean means a line-lock like the racers use. Not the best way to rig up a parking brake.<P>I did the Grand Am rotor and caliper swap, front and back, but still have the original Fiero master cylinder (for about a year now). Since the caliper pistons are bigger, they use a larger volume of brake fluid than the stock Fiero ones which result in a lower brake pedal than normal at full stop. I found that it works ok though, probably would be better to upgrade the M/C too if you do front and rear. However, if you only swap the front brakes with Grand AM parts, then you should be just fine with the Fiero M/C, that's my opinion.||||reg||1
Z||000011||MrPBody||10-27-2000||11:11 PM||||You might want to see  <A HREF="http://fierozone.tripod.com/realbrakes/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fierozone.tripod.com/realbrakes/index.html</A> <P>(a thorough description of why and how)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Seanh||10-28-2000||12:45 AM||||That is the site I have been looking at all along. This is what I was talking about as far as an in-line brake booster:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/img0.gif"> <BR>I am thinking now that I dont want berettas on the rear unless I can get the cal's to use the stock e-brake cables.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000013||Seanh||10-28-2000||12:49 AM||||Would you email me that web page you have saved? I have a virus on my computer so I am not emailing anyone from home. I am almost done with my website, so I could post this information on my website (I would give him the credit) until we find his page. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000014||mrfixit58||10-28-2000||09:43 AM||||Be careful using any kind of rear brake lock that requires the rear brakes to lock with hydraulic pressure. If the hydraulics are left under pressure for an extended period of time, you run the risk of a fluid leak, or a stuck seal. The stock Fiero rear calipers use a mechanical lever and cam that mechanically pushes the piston out and locks the brakes. <P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000015||bHooper||10-28-2000||11:34 AM||||With the GA/Beretta brakes, do you use the rears for the back of the Fiero, or th Front's on the back?<P>Doesn't the rear GA brakes have a mounting for brake cables?<P>just curious.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000016||batboy||10-28-2000||11:51 AM||||Don't quote me exactly on the years, but the common brake swap uses FRONT calipers and rotors from the 85-90 Grand Am/Beretta for both front and rear on the Fiero. These GA cars still had rear drum brakes. The newer GA (at least the GT) cars have rear disc, but I don't know how adaptable they are or if anyone has even tried yet. The appeal of the late 80's GA brake swap is that the parts are so super cheap and readily available.<P>By the way, I just always asked for 1988 Grand Am parts and everything fit just fine. I paid a couple bucks a piece more for American made rotors, because the Chinese ones looked poorly made and I've heard the steel is inferior too. I also paid slightly more and bought all new (not rebuilt) calipers. Everything was still cheaper than a complete brake job using Fiero parts, plus the bonus of having better brakes.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 10-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||theogre||10-28-2000||01:24 PM||||I've been digging around on this allot lately. I'm in need of brakes and it was time for a desicion.<P>I will not be doing any type of upgrade to the rear brakes. I don't believe there is any need unless you are racing regularly or have a big V6 or V8 upgrade. A big motor only really becomes an issue if you have an automatic tranny. In an automatic car the engine loads the brakes durring stopping unlike a stick where engine load is disconnected durring stopping.<P>On the other hand the fronts have to go. Fiero isn't the first GM car I've had with these solid rotors up front. Used to have a Chevy Monza with them in the late 70's early 80's. It had all the same problems. namely brake fade in heavy traffic or going down a grade into a sharp turn and rotor warp from the high heat.<P>I'll be doing the grandam/berreta based upgrade to the front & using an adjustable prop valve. It's cheap and uses readily available parts. Gives improved braking. Solves the majority of problems with solid rotors. It also allows the stock rims to be used which is an issue with some of the other kits around.<P><BR>As I've said many times now.... Cross Drill and slotting is primarily a waste management device not a cooling method. These techniques are used to remove outgas and dust from the pads. The cooling effect of either is negligable. It's performance benefit to most street driven cars is questionable. <P>Either process requires very carfull analysis of the rotor and metalurgy of it before machining. If the rotors are not drilled or slotted correctly they will crack and that can cause explosive failure. This is not something I trust to most people. I'm not even sure I trust Fiero store with this one.<P>Using a line lock for a parking brake is not a good idea. I drove wreckers for a number of years all of which had line locks. Wheel cylinders left under pressure for long period of time will often seep out fluid and let loose on the pads/shoes. We used the line locks only as an aide for loading or winching to get a better bite of the wheels. We never trusted them to be a parking brake because either the lock would leak or the wheels would leak if you left it overnight.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||Seanh||10-28-2000||02:47 PM||||I am starting to think I should just do Beretta brakes on the front and get some new pads for the rear. I am not planning on a V8 conversion, but I am at least going to do a 3.2, probally a 3.4 if I can find one. But, the car is an automatic (4spd). I can see why that line lock parking brake would probally not be a good idea. Is there any way to upgrade for the rear using the beretta/grand am brakes? I mean, a caliper that has the same parking brake design?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000019||bHooper||10-28-2000||03:20 PM||||one option, would be to switch over to the GA brakes on the rear, if /when you were racing, and back for street driving.<P>i am thinking of doing this.  with the cost of the GA calipers and rotors, it is not that expensive!<P>so, next question, how good are the rebuild kits for the fiero rear calipers?  <P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000020||Seanh||10-28-2000||04:37 PM||||I was looking at that page robertjay sent me. His calculations call for 11.26" rotors and require 16" rims at least. He talks about useing 10.5" rotors and how to calculate the mounting bracket designes. I am wondering, Could I do the beretta on the front like I was planning, useing the stock sized rotors, and then do the rears with 10.5" rotors and Caddy calipers? Would 10.5" rotors work on stock 15" rims? I am probally just going to do the fronts like I planned and then see if I really even need to do the rears.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Monkeyman||10-28-2000||07:00 PM||||I keep hearing the same question asked.  "Isn't there a provision for an E brake on the GA calipers?"  No.  You have to use the <I>front</I> GA brakes on the rear of the Fiero.  The generation GA/Beretta that these cheap brakes came from had drum brakes in the rear and the parking brake was a part of that.  If you go with GA brakes on the rear, you won't have an E brake.||||reg||1
Z||000022||batboy||10-28-2000||07:06 PM||||Even for SCCA racing, if you're staying with V6 power, I doubt you'd need anything better than 10.5" rotors. The calipers that fit those rotors have significantly bigger pistons than even the Grand Am ones.<P>There were (maybe still are) several big brake kits on the market. I think Ryane Motor Sports and others carried this. They had fabricated a caliper adaptor mounting bracket allowing bigger calipers to mount on the Fiero hubs. Like the Grand Am swap, you have to have front hubs on the pre-88 cars.<P>I believe they used 10.5" S-10 4X4 front brakes on the front of the Fiero and 10.5" 1986 Z-28 IROC rear brakes on the rear of the Fiero. The IROC calipers have e-brake levers. But there was something about having to redo the cables because maybe the lever pulled the wrong way or something. I don't remember for sure.<P>Somewhere I saved a photo of the adaptor brackets and had toyed around with the idea of making a cad pattern off the pic and trying to make my own brackets. I figured you'd need a real high grade plate steel to begin with and since I didn't see anything useable in my scrap pile, I kind of lost interest.||||reg||1
Z||000023||batboy||10-28-2000||07:09 PM||||PS: If this is a street Fiero, listen to Ogre, he speaks wisely. Even with a V8 Fiero, I find the GA brakes work just fine.<P>I'd still like to find a cheap 1999 Grand Am GT that was wrecked, but has a good, low mileage drivetrain and chassis. Using that as a donor car, I'd figure out a way to transfer as much to my poor dead 87 SE as possible. Including engine, tranny, wiring, brakes, wheels, and maybe even some of the optional toys, bells, and whistles. I'm really getting a wild hair about this idea. Maybe next year I'll keep my eye open for a good donor car.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 10-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||theogre||10-29-2000||12:05 PM||||I don't know any more about this than what's in the thread. I intend to check it out and rebuild both of mine. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20000503-2-001095.html" TARGET=_blank>rear rebuild kit thread.</A> ||||reg||1
